The bug is that `Arrays.sort` on a `char[]` sorts in place and returns `void`, so `Arrays.sort(a).equals(...)` wouldn't even compile. If it's compiling and returning false, the likely culprit is comparing the two arrays with `equals` instead of `Arrays.equals`, since array `equals` is reference equality and always false for two different arrays.

```java
boolean isAnagram(String s, String t) {
    if (s.length() != t.length()) return false;
    char[] a = s.toCharArray();
    char[] b = t.toCharArray();
    Arrays.sort(a);
    Arrays.sort(b);
    return Arrays.equals(a, b);
}
```

Sorting both and comparing with `Arrays.equals` works because anagrams have identical sorted character sequences, and the length check short-circuits the obvious mismatch.

